<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's a Square Cracker. – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes on Our Plates</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's a Square Cracker.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Recognize familiar words in simple texts.<br>Reading: Identify simple words for very common symbols.<br>Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can point to and name familiar food and shape-related words in simple texts (e.g., recognizing the word "apple" in the sentence, "The apple is round.").<br>Can identify simple words related to shapes (e.g., pointing to a cracker and saying "square").<br>Can recognize sounds of letters related to the names of food items when supported with visuals (e.g., identifying the /ɔ/ sound in "orange" along with the letter O, or the /b/ sound in "banana" when shown its crescent shape).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays large flashcards or realia of food items from Lesson 1, focusing on "cracker," "apple," "banana," "orange," and their shapes. Teacher says, "Look! Food!" and points to a round apple. "This is an apple. It is round." Then points to a square cracker. "This is a cracker. It is square." Students repeat chorally. The teacher also shows the word cards for 'round' and 'square'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard showing a "square cracker" and the word "cracker" beneath it. The teacher slowly reads the word "cracker" aloud, tracing the word with a finger, emphasizing the /k/ sound. Then points to the shape and says "square", tracing the word "square" and emphasizing the /r/ sound. The teacher models pointing to the word "cracker" and then to the cracker, saying, "This is a cracker." The teacher then models pointing to the word "square" and saying, "This is square."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a round orange) "Is this a square cracker?" (Students shake heads 'no' or give thumbs down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a square cracker) "What is this? Is it a cracker or an apple?" (Students point to 'cracker' or say "cracker").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center;">(5-7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, illustrated chart or whiteboard display titled "My Food Shapes." It features simple sentences with corresponding pictures for different food items and their shapes. Each sentence is short and uses large, clear font.
                    Example sentences:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This is a <img src="placeholder_apple.png" alt="apple" width="30" height="30"> apple. It is <img src="placeholder_round.png" alt="round shape" width="30" height="30"> round.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This is a <img src="placeholder_cracker.png" alt="cracker" width="30" height="30"> cracker. It is <img src="placeholder_square.png" alt="square shape" width="30" height="30"> square.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This is a <img src="placeholder_banana.png" alt="banana" width="30" height="30"> banana. It is <img src="placeholder_oval.png" alt="oval shape" width="30" height="30"> oval.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This is a <img src="placeholder_orange.png" alt="orange" width="30" height="30"> orange. It is <img src="placeholder_round.png" alt="round shape" width="30" height="30"> round.</li>
                    </ul>
                    The genre is descriptive text, simple register, and visual format.
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying food shapes and asking about shapes in food.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> The teacher reads each sentence aloud, pointing to the words and pictures. Students listen and then point to the correct food item as the teacher asks, "What is this food?" (e.g., pointing to the apple picture).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads a statement and students show a 'thumbs up' for true or 'thumbs down' for false. For example, teacher points to the cracker and says, "The cracker is round." (Students show thumbs down). Teacher points to the apple and says, "The apple is round." (Students show thumbs up).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher asks, "Show me 'square'!" and students point to the word "square" on the chart. "Show me 'apple'!" and students point to the word "apple."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center;">(10-12 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading familiar words (nouns, adjectives), identifying simple words for shapes, recognizing letter sounds.
                    <b>Grammar:</b> Adjective + noun (e.g., "round apple"), Present simple (e.g., "Oranges are round.").
                    <b>Vocabulary:</b> pineapple, lemon, lime, mango, papaya, cracker, banana, food, round, square, oval, big, small, healthy, tasty, orange.
                    <b>Pronunciation:</b> /r/ in "round," /k/ in "cracker."
                    <b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Recognizing sounds of letters of the alphabet in names of shapes and foods (e.g., /æ/ in "apple", /b/ in "banana", /ɔ/ in "orange").
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Word-Picture Match):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with pictures of a cracker, apple, banana, orange, and pineapple. Next to each picture are three word options (e.g., for cracker: "apple," "cracker," "banana"). Students circle or point to the correct word that matches the picture. The teacher models the first one: "Look at the cracker. Is it 'apple' or 'cracker'? Yes, 'cracker'!" (Teacher circles 'cracker' on a projected worksheet).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice (Shape Word Identification):</b> The teacher shows flashcards with simple sentences like "The apple is round." or "It's a square cracker." and asks students to point to or underline the shape word ("round," "square"). The teacher might say, "Find 'round'!" or "Point to 'square'!" on their worksheets or individual cards. Students also identify the food word.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Phonemic Awareness (Initial Sound Match):</b> The teacher shows a picture of an orange. "This is an orange. /ɔ/ /ɔ/ orange." The teacher then shows letter cards O, B, A. "Which letter says /ɔ/?" Students point to the letter O. Repeat for "banana" /b/ and "apple" /æ/ using letters B and A. The teacher guides students to trace the letter shapes with their fingers.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center;">(15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shapes on Our Plates")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Read and Find!" – A guided reading activity in small groups (2-3 students) or individually. Each student receives a set of picture cards showing various food items (apple, cracker, banana, orange, pineapple, lemon, mango) and shape cards (round, square, oval). They also receive a small set of simple sentence strips or word cards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students are given simple reading prompts like "Find a round apple." or "Show me a square cracker." or "The banana is oval." The teacher monitors, offering help with pronunciation and word recognition. The goal is for students to read the prompt (either a single word or a short phrase/sentence) and then physically identify and show the corresponding picture or shape card. This connects directly to the theme "It's a Square Cracker." and scenario "Shapes on Our Plates" by having students read about specific food shapes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in recognizing familiar words in written form, collaborative interaction (if in pairs/groups) in pointing to and confirming the correct items, and successful completion of the reading identification task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Read and Find!" activity and a quick individual check. The teacher notes which students can consistently point to familiar food and shape words when presented in isolation or in a very simple sentence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Individual "Point and Read" check. The teacher shows students 3-4 word cards (e.g., "cracker," "round," "apple," "square") and asks them to point to each word as the teacher says it, and then for students to attempt to say the word after the teacher models it one last time. For phonemic awareness, the teacher shows a letter (e.g., 'A') and asks, "What sound does 'A' make in 'apple'?" or "What letter is for 'banana' /b/?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to recognize and point to target vocabulary words (e.g., "cracker," "apple," "round," "square") in written form.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify simple words related to shapes (e.g., pointing to "square").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to identify sounds of initial letters of food names (e.g., /æ/ in "apple" with letter A).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are shown two large cards: one with a happy face and "I can read food words!" and one with a neutral face and "I need more help." They point to the face that best describes how they feel about reading food words today. The teacher also asks, "Did I see the word 'square'?" (Students give a thumbs up or down). "Did I see the word 'apple'?" (Students give a thumbs up or down).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new food word did you read today? (Students point to a word card or picture.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What shape word did you see? (Students point to a word card or picture.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What specific reading strategies worked well for the 1st graders (e.g., tracing words, picture-word matching, large print)? Which students struggled with word recognition and what scaffolds (e.g., more direct teacher modeling, flashcard repetition) will be needed for future lessons? How can the phonemic awareness practice be made more engaging or effective? Were the instructions clear and concise enough for this age group?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This reading lesson helped students recognize familiar food and shape words. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will actively use these words to describe food shapes to each other, building on their visual recognition to oral production and interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center;">(5 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>